.TH BOND\-MANAGER 8 "2026-09-25" "bond\-manager 3.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
bond\-manager \- safe NetworkManager bond management for RHEL-like systems
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bond\-manager
.RI [ "GLOBAL FLAGS" ]
.I command
.RI [ ARGS ]
.br
.B bond\-manager
.RS 4
(with no command on a terminal: the guided menus)
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bond\-manager
creates, modifies, repairs and removes Linux kernel bonding interfaces
through NetworkManager on RHEL-like systems (RHEL, CentOS Stream, Rocky,
Alma, Fedora).
It is designed for remote operation over SSH on production machines:
every change is executed as a planned transaction and is protected so
that a mistake \(em including one that severs the SSH session driving
the change \(em rolls the network back to its previous state
automatically.
.PP
A mutating command runs as:
plan \(-> snapshot \(-> arm protection \(-> execute \(-> verify \(->
commit or roll back.
The plan is rendered before anything is executed; with
.B \-\-dry\-run
the rendering is all that happens.
Verification checks kernel state
.RI ( /proc ", " /sys )
and the routing table rather than what
.BR nmcli (1)
reports.
See
.B SAFETY MODEL
below for the protection tiers.
.PP
Run with no command on a terminal,
.B bond\-manager
starts guided menus covering the same workflows (see
.BR "INTERACTIVE MENUS" );
each change shows its command-line equivalent so it can be scripted
afterwards.
Newcomers: start with
.B bond\-manager help basics
or the beginner's guide,
.IR docs/GUIDE.md .
.PP
All mutating invocations are serialized through a single lock (exit code 4
when another instance holds it), and all of them honor
.BR \-\-dry\-run .
This covers the change commands and the safety commands alike:
.BR commit ,
.BR rollback ,
.BR "snapshot create" ,
.B "snapshot restore"
and
.B "snapshot prune"
take the lock and write nothing under
.BR \-\-dry\-run .
The read-only commands \(em including
.BR "snapshot list" ", " "snapshot diff" " and " verify
\(em take no lock and write nothing at all.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Render the plan; execute nothing, write nothing.
Dry runs need neither root nor a running NetworkManager.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
No prompts; auto-commit when verification passes.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable output.
Applies to
.BR list ", " show " and " status
only;
.B doctor
has no JSON form.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Mirror log records to stderr.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Suppress progress messages.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable colored output (the
.B NO_COLOR
environment variable is also honored).
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Plain numbered menus and prompts instead of arrow-key menus (serial
consoles, screen readers, basic terminals).
.TP
.BI \-\-rollback\-window " SECONDS"
Auto-rollback window for unconfirmed changes, 10..86400 seconds
(default: the
.B ROLLBACK_WINDOW
config key, 120 seconds out of the box).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-checkpoint
Skip NetworkManager D-Bus checkpoints and fall back to the
deadman/snapshot tiers (see
.BR "SAFETY MODEL" ).
.TP
.B \-\-force\-unsafe
Allow touching the device that carries the current SSH session without
checkpoint protection.
Without this flag such changes are refused.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
After a command
.RB ( "bond\-manager swap\-member \-\-help" ),
print that command's help instead.
.TP
.BR \-\-status ", " \-\-export\-json " \fIPATH\fP"
Legacy v2.x compatibility flags:
.B \-\-status
is equivalent to the
.B status
command, and
.BI \-\-export\-json " PATH"
writes the JSON inventory document to
.IR PATH .
Combined
.RB ( "\-\-status \-\-export\-json " PATH ),
the file is written, the human-readable status is printed, and the exit
status is the health verdict (0, 10 or 11) \(em not 0 \(em so a monitoring
caller still learns that a bond is degraded or down.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Read-only commands (no root required; write nothing)
.TP
.B list
One line per bond: name, mode, health verdict.
.TP
.BI show " BOND"
Full detail for one bond: mode, health with reasons, miimon, active and
primary member, per-member MII status / speed / duplex / link failure
count, addresses and VLANs.
Supports
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.BR status " [\fIBOND\fP]"
Health summary for one bond or all bonds.
Exit status encodes the worst verdict: 0 healthy, 10 degraded, 11 down.
Supports
.BR \-\-json ,
which honors the
.I BOND
argument:
.B status BOND \-\-json
emits a
.B bonds
array holding only that bond, while
.B status \-\-json
emits the full inventory document.
.TP
.BI diagnose " BOND" " " [ \-\-extended "] [" "\-\-target IP" ]
Run diagnostics against a bond: link and bonding state, and connectivity
probing.
.B \-\-extended
adds deeper checks;
.BI \-\-target " IP"
sets the ping target (default: the default gateway).
.TP
.B doctor
Environment preflight: required and optional tools, NetworkManager
state, bonding module, SELinux, config presence, the protection tier
this host supports, and any pending (unconfirmed) change.
Exit status 3 when the host is not ready.
.TP
.BR nics " [" \-\-all ]
List the network ports with their link state, speed, bond membership and
addresses, and a plain verdict per port: free and usable, no link (cable
or switch port), has an IP (probably in use), or carries the current SSH
connection.
Ports hidden by the NIC policy are counted, and listed with
.BR \-\-all .
Never calls
.BR nmcli .
.TP
.BR help " [" \fICOMMAND\fP | \fITOPIC\fP ]
Plain-English help.
With a command: what it does, usage, copy-paste examples (always starting
with a
.B \-n
preview) and pitfalls.
Topics:
.BR basics ", " modes ", " lacp ", " safety ", " practice ", " moving ,
.BR glossary ", " keys ", " exit\-codes .
Exit status 2 for an unknown word, with a suggestion.
.TP
.BR "config show" " | " "config path"
Print the effective configuration (defaults overlaid with
.IR /etc/bond_manager.conf ),
or the config file path.
.TP
.B completion bash
Emit a bash completion script
(typically installed with:
.BR "bond\-manager completion bash > /etc/bash_completion.d/bond\-manager" ).
.SS Change commands (root required; guarded by plan \(-> snapshot \(-> checkpoint \(-> verify)
.TP
.BI create " BOND" " \-\-mode " MODE " \-\-members " IF1,IF2 " " [ options ]
Create a bond with the given members.
Accepts the IP and tuning flags listed below, plus repeatable
.BI \-\-vlan " VID[:ip4=..;gw4=..]"
to create VLANs on top of the new bond in the same transaction.
.TP
.BI modify " BOND" " " [ "\-\-mode MODE" "] [" "\-\-opt k=v" "]... [" "\-\-del\-opt k" "]... [" "ip/mtu flags" ]
Modify an existing bond: mode, bonding options, IP configuration, MTU.
Only the difference from the current profile is planned.
When the profile carries no explicit
.B mode=
in its
.BR bond.options ,
the bond is treated as
.B balance\-rr
\(em the kernel and NetworkManager default \(em and a note says so, so the
resulting option set is validated against the mode the bond really runs in.
.TP
.BI add\-member " BOND IF" [ ,IF... ]
Add member interfaces to a bond.
.TP
.BI remove\-member " BOND IF" [ ,IF... ]
Remove member interfaces from a bond.
Verification asserts the named members are no longer enslaved.
Removing the last member takes the bond down: that is confirmed
interactively and verified with a relaxed state expectation, so a correct
change is not rolled back for leaving the bond down.
.TP
.BI swap\-member " BOND" " \-\-old " IF " \-\-new " IF
Replace one member NIC with another: the new member is added first, then
the old one is removed, so redundancy is never reduced below the
starting point.
Verification covers both halves: the new member enslaved, the old one gone.
.TP
.BI remove " BOND" " " [ \-\-keep\-vlans ]
Delete a bond together with its member and VLAN profiles.
.B \-\-keep\-vlans
leaves the VLAN profiles in place.
.RB ( delete
is accepted as an alias.)
.TP
.BI "vlan add" " BOND VID" [ :ip4=..;gw4=.. ]
Create a VLAN interface on top of a bond, optionally with IPv4
configuration in the token.
.TP
.BI "vlan modify" " BOND VID" " " [ "ip flags" ]
Change the IP configuration of an existing VLAN interface.
.TP
.BI "vlan remove" " BOND VID"
Delete a VLAN interface from a bond.
.TP
.BI "vlan list" " BOND"
List the VLAN interfaces configured on a bond.
.TP
.BI clone " SRC DST" " \-\-members " IF [ ,IF... ] " " [ \-\-copy\-ip "] [" \-\-copy\-vlans ]
Create bond
.I DST
on new member NICs using the mode and bonding options of
.IR SRC ,
optionally copying its IP configuration and cloning its VLANs.
.TP
.BI repair " BOND"
Rebuild member port profiles from kernel state \(em for bonds whose
NetworkManager profiles were deleted or mangled while the kernel bond
still runs.
.TP
.BI verify " BOND"
Re-run the verification checks against the current state of a bond and
render the pass/warn/fail report.
Read-only: no root, no lock, nothing written.
Exit status 1 when a check fails.
.SS Create/modify IP and tuning flags
.TP
.BI \-\-ip4 " dhcp\fR|\fPnone\fR|\fPCIDR" [ ,CIDR ]
IPv4 method or static address list:
.B dhcp
sets
.BR "ipv4.method auto" ,
.B none
sets
.BR "ipv4.method disabled" ,
and an address list sets
.B ipv4.method manual
with
.BR ipv4.addresses .
.B dhcp
and
.B none
also clear a previous fixed address and gateway, which NetworkManager would
otherwise keep.
.TP
.BI \-\-gw4 " ADDR\fR|\fPnone"
IPv4 gateway;
.B none
removes it.
Without this flag,
.B modify
keeps the current gateway.
.TP
.BI \-\-dns4 " A,B\fR|\fPnone"
IPv4 DNS servers;
.B none
removes them.
.TP
.BI \-\-ip6 " auto\fR|\fPdhcp\fR|\fPnone\fR|\fPCIDR" [ ,CIDR ]
IPv6 method or static address list.
.B auto
sets
.B ipv6.method auto
(SLAAC), while
.B dhcp
sets
.B ipv6.method dhcp
\(em DHCPv6 without SLAAC, a distinct NetworkManager method, not a synonym
for
.BR auto .
.B none
sets
.BR "ipv6.method ignore" :
the
.B disabled
value exists only from NetworkManager 1.20, and
.B ignore
means the same thing on every release this tool supports.
.TP
.BI \-\-gw6 " ADDR\fR|\fPnone"
IPv6 gateway;
.B none
removes it.
.TP
.BI \-\-dns6 " A,B\fR|\fPnone"
IPv6 DNS servers;
.B none
removes them.
.TP
.BI \-\-mtu " N"
MTU for the bond (and its members).
.TP
.BI \-\-opt " key=value"
Set an arbitrary bonding option (repeatable).
Values may contain commas
.RB ( "\-\-opt arp_ip_target=10.0.0.1,10.0.0.2" );
they are merged into the existing
.B bond.options
as one option, not split into bogus keys.
.TP
.BI \-\-del\-opt " key"
Delete a bonding option (repeatable;
.B modify
only).
.TP
.BI \-\-vlan " VID" [ :ip4=..;gw4=.. ]
Create a VLAN on the bond in the same transaction (repeatable;
.B create
only).
.TP
.BI \-\-miimon " MS"
MII link monitoring interval in milliseconds.
.TP
.BI \-\-primary " IF"
Primary member (active\-backup).
.TP
.BI \-\-lacp\-rate " fast\fR|\fPslow"
LACPDU rate (802.3ad).
.TP
.BI \-\-xmit\-hash " POLICY"
Transmit hash policy (802.3ad / balance\-xor), e.g.
.BR layer3+4 .
.TP
.BI \-\-arp\-interval " MS"
ARP monitoring interval in milliseconds.
.TP
.BI \-\-arp\-targets " IP" [ ,IP ]
ARP monitoring target addresses.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-links " N"
Minimum number of active links for the bond to be considered up.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-activate
Write profiles but do not activate the bond.
Verification then runs with a relaxed state expectation, since the bond is
legitimately down afterwards.
.PP
Supported bond modes:
.BR balance\-rr ,
.BR active\-backup ,
.BR balance\-xor ,
.BR broadcast ,
.BR 802.3ad ,
.BR balance\-tlb ,
.BR balance\-alb .
.SS Safety commands
.TP
.B commit
Confirm a pending change: keep the applied state and disarm the
auto-rollback protection.
Can be run from a new session (for example after an SSH reconnect).
.IP
If the NetworkManager checkpoint can no longer be destroyed because it has
already gone, the change was most likely rolled back server-side when the
window expired.
.B commit
reports that and exits 5; it never reports such a change as committed.
Confirm the actual state with
.BR "bond\-manager status" .
.TP
.BR rollback " [\fB\-\-snapshot\fP \fIID\fP]"
Revert a pending change through whichever protection tier is armed, or
\(em when no change is pending \(em restore a snapshot
(the most recent one, or the one named with
.BR \-\-snapshot ).
Restoring a named snapshot while a change is pending disarms that
change's protection first, so no checkpoint or timer can fire later on top
of the restored profiles.
.TP
.B snapshot create
Take a manual snapshot of the NetworkManager connection profiles in
.B both
profile stores: the keyfile store
.RI ( /etc/NetworkManager/system\-connections )
and the ifcfg store
.RI ( /etc/sysconfig/network\-scripts ,
used by RHEL 8's
.B ifcfg\-rh
plugin).
In the ifcfg store only NetworkManager's own files are captured \(em
.IR ifcfg\-* ", " keys\-* ", " route\-* ", " route6\-* ", " rule\-*
and
.I rule6\-*
at the top level \(em never the legacy
.IR ifup* / ifdown*
scripts that share the directory.
.TP
.B snapshot list
List snapshots with ID, creation time and reason.
Read-only.
.TP
.BI "snapshot diff" " ID"
Show what would change if the snapshot were restored, for both stores.
Read-only.
.TP
.BR "snapshot restore" " [\fIID\fP]"
Restore a snapshot (the most recent when
.I ID
is omitted).
The restore reconciles: files created after the snapshot are deleted
(with confirmation), in both stores, so restoring undoes creates, not just
edits.
The current state is snapshotted first, and if extraction fails the
command fails loudly, naming that pre-restore snapshot \(em it does not
report success over a half-restored profile set.
With no snapshots present the command stops with a precondition error
(exit 3).
.TP
.B snapshot prune
Delete old snapshots beyond the configured
.B MAX_BACKUPS
(default 10), oldest first.
Snapshots that are in use are never deleted: the one named in the
pending-change state file, the one a restore is currently reading, and the
one just created.
Protected snapshots do not consume the
.B MAX_BACKUPS
budget, so pinning one does not shorten retention for the rest.
.TP
.BR bundle " [\fB\-\-output\fP \fIPATH\fP] [\fB\-\-redact\fP]"
Create a support bundle (logs, state, diagnostics).
.B \-\-redact
strips IP and MAC addresses for off-site tickets.
.TP
.B init
Install the default configuration file and logrotate policy, and create
the backup and support directories.
.SH INTERACTIVE MENUS
With no command on a terminal (or with the
.B tui
command),
.B bond\-manager
shows a dashboard of every bond \(em health, ports, the port carrying the
current SSH session, the protection tier, and any change waiting to be
committed, with its countdown \(em and a menu of jobs:
check bonds, move a bond to a new switch (swap members one at a time),
build a bond, change a bond (ports, mode, preferred port, IP, MTU, VLANs,
options, clone, delete), fix drifted profiles, undo and snapshots, tools,
and help.
The menus need no extra packages.
.PP
Every job goes through the same workflow code as the command line.
Choices are picked from lists; typed values are validated as they are
entered.
Before anything runs, a review screen summarises the change, warns when it
touches the SSH session's device, and shows the equivalent command line;
the plan and the commit countdown then appear exactly as on the command
line.
.PP
Practice mode (key
.BR p )
runs every job as a dry run.
It is forced on when changes are impossible (not root, NetworkManager not
running), and is on from the start with
.BR \-n .
.B \-y
is ignored in the menus.
.PP
Keys: Up/Down or
.BR j / k
move, Enter chooses,
.BR 1 \(en 9
choose by number, Esc or
.B q
go back, Space ticks a check box,
.BR a / n
tick all/none;
on the home screen
.B p
toggles practice mode,
.B r
refreshes,
.B ?
opens help and
.B q
quits (with a warning if a change is still waiting).
In plain mode, type the number and press Enter.
.SH SAFETY MODEL
Every change is protected by up to three tiers; the strongest available
tier is chosen automatically
.RB ( doctor
reports which one this host supports):
.TP
.B Tier 1: checkpoint
A NetworkManager D-Bus checkpoint (created via
.BR busctl )
with a server-side rollback timeout.
NetworkManager itself reverts device and profile state if the change is
never confirmed \(em safe even when the change severs the SSH session
that is driving it.
Checkpoints are created with flags that delete new connections and
disconnect new devices on rollback, so a rollback undoes a
.IR create ,
not just an edit.
.TP
.B Tier 2: deadman
When no D-Bus checkpoint is available (or
.B \-\-no\-checkpoint
was given), a transient systemd timer
.RB ( systemd\-run )
runs
.B bond\-manager rollback
against the pre-change snapshot unless it is cancelled in time by
.BR commit .
After restoring the profiles it brings the affected bonds, their ports and
VLANs up again with the restored settings, and deletes a bond or VLAN the
change created, so a change that cut the session is really undone.
.TP
.B Tier 3: snapshot
A tar snapshot of all connection profiles in both stores, with a manifest.
This tier is always in effect \(em a snapshot is taken before every
change regardless of the higher tiers \(em but on hosts with neither
.B busctl
nor
.BR systemd\-run ,
rollback is manual
.RB ( "bond\-manager rollback" ):
nothing reverts the change on its own, and the tool says so rather than
promising an auto-rollback it cannot deliver.
.PP
A deadman timer is only armed when the path bond\-manager was invoked as is
an executable file, since systemd has to re-run it; otherwise the tool
falls back to tier 3 instead of arming protection that could never fire.
.PP
After the change is applied, a verification gate checks the result
against kernel state
.RI ( /proc/net/bonding ", " /sys )
and the routing table.
Checks that FAIL \(em bond absent from the kernel, operstate not reaching
.BR up ,
wrong mode, an expected member not enslaved, a removed member still
enslaved \(em roll the change back immediately and the command exits 5.
Checks that only warn \(em a member's MII status, a missing LACP partner,
no currently-active member, no address yet, an unanswered gateway ping
\(em are printed for the operator's decision and never roll anything back.
Operations that legitimately leave the bond down
.RB ( \-\-no\-activate ,
removing the last member) are verified with a relaxed state expectation.
.PP
If verification passes, the auto-rollback window is re-budgeted to a full
window \(em the time the apply itself consumed does not come out of the
decision time \(em and the change is committed automatically with
.BR \-\-yes ;
otherwise the operator is prompted with a countdown \(em
.B K
keeps the change,
.B U
undoes it,
.B E
adds five minutes on the checkpoint tier
.RB ( c ", " r ", " e
are accepted too) \(em and an unconfirmed change is rolled back
automatically when the rollback window expires.
On the deadman tier the prompt performs that rollback itself when the
window runs out, since it still holds the lock the timer would need.
With no terminal to ask on, or when the terminal goes away during the
countdown, protection stays armed and the command exits 6.
.PP
Pending-change state is kept in
.IR /run/bond\-manager/pending.state ,
so a
.B new
session \(em for instance after an SSH disconnect \(em can run
.B bond\-manager commit
or
.B bond\-manager rollback
to resolve it.
Changes that would touch the device carrying the current SSH session are
refused unless checkpoint protection is active or
.B \-\-force\-unsafe
is given.
The devices considered are the bond, its current members and its VLAN
interfaces.
The session's device is resolved from
.BR SSH_CONNECTION ,
falling back to
.B SSH_CLIENT
and then to the
.BR who (1)
login record for the session's terminal, because
.BR sudo (8)
strips
.B SSH_CONNECTION
under its default
.BR env_reset .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, healthy, or nothing to do.
.TP
.B 1
Error.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error (unknown command, missing or invalid arguments, or
.B help
with a word it does not know).
.TP
.B 3
Precondition failed (not root, NetworkManager not active, missing tool,
no pending change, ...).
.TP
.B 4
Locked: another bond\-manager instance is running. (A change waiting at its
commit gate does not hold the lock, so
.B commit
and
.B rollback
work from another session.)
.TP
.B 5
The change failed verification and was rolled back.
Also returned by
.B commit
when the checkpoint had already been destroyed, meaning NetworkManager
most likely rolled the change back when the window expired.
.TP
.B 6
The change was applied but is unconfirmed (pending); it will be rolled
back automatically unless committed within the rollback window.
.TP
.B 10
At least one bond is degraded
.RB ( status ).
.TP
.B 11
At least one bond is down
.RB ( status ).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Every filesystem root the tool reads or writes can be overridden, which
is how the test suite and fixture-based inspection work:
.BR BM_PROC_ROOT " (default " /proc ),
.BR BM_SYS_ROOT " (" /sys ),
.BR BM_CONN_DIR " (" /etc/NetworkManager/system\-connections ),
.BR BM_IFCFG_DIR " (" /etc/sysconfig/network\-scripts ),
.BR BM_CONF ,
.BR BM_LOG_FILE ,
.BR BM_BACKUP_DIR ,
.BR BM_SUPPORT_DIR ,
.BR BM_RUN_DIR ,
.BR BM_LOGROTATE_CONF
(defaults for these are listed under
.BR FILES ).
.PP
.B NO_COLOR
disables colored output.
.B BM_ASCII=1
draws the menus with ASCII instead of box-drawing characters (automatic
without a UTF-8 locale or on the Linux console).
.B BM_PLAIN=1
is the same as
.BR \-\-plain .
Plain menus are also used automatically when
.B TERM
is
.B dumb
or unset, on serial consoles, and when input or output is not a terminal.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/bond_manager.conf
Configuration.
The file is parsed line by line against a key allowlist
.RB ( "KEY=\[dq]value\[dq]" " per line)"
and never sourced; unknown keys and invalid values are ignored with a
warning.
See
.B bond\-manager config show
for the effective values and
.B bond\-manager init
to install the commented default file.
.TP
.I /var/log/bond_manager.log
Structured operation log.
Read-only commands never write it.
.TP
.I /etc/logrotate.d/bond_manager
Logrotate policy for the log file (installed by
.BR init ).
.TP
.I /var/backups/bond_manager/
Snapshot archives and manifests:
.I conn\-ID.tar.gz
(keyfile store),
.I conn\-ID.ifcfg.tar.gz
(ifcfg store, written only when that store holds NetworkManager files) and
.I conn\-ID.manifest
(sha256 of every captured file in both stores, plus the directories they
came from).
.TP
.I /var/log/bond_manager/support/
Support bundles created by
.BR bundle .
.TP
.I /run/bond\-manager/
Runtime state: the mutation lock and
.IR pending.state .
.TP
.I /etc/NetworkManager/system\-connections/
The keyfile connection profiles (RHEL 9 and later) that snapshots capture
and restore.
.TP
.I /etc/sysconfig/network\-scripts/
The ifcfg connection profiles written by NetworkManager's
.B ifcfg\-rh
plugin (the default on RHEL 8).
Snapshots capture and restore only
.IR ifcfg\-* ", " keys\-* ", " route\-* ", " route6\-* ", " rule\-*
and
.I rule6\-*
at the top level of this directory; the legacy
.IR ifup* / ifdown*
scripts and helper files that also live there are never read or written.
.SH EXAMPLES
Find the free ports, and read the help for a command:
.PP
.nf
.RS
bond\-manager nics
bond\-manager help swap\-member
.RE
.fi
.PP
Create an LACP bond with a static address and jumbo frames:
.PP
.nf
.RS
bond\-manager create bond0 \-\-mode 802.3ad \e
    \-\-members ens1f0,ens1f1 \e
    \-\-ip4 10.0.0.10/24 \-\-gw4 10.0.0.1 \-\-dns4 10.0.0.2,10.0.0.3 \e
    \-\-mtu 9000 \-\-lacp\-rate fast \-\-xmit\-hash layer3+4
.RE
.fi
.PP
Preview the same change without touching anything:
.PP
.nf
.RS
bond\-manager \-\-dry\-run create bond0 \-\-mode 802.3ad \-\-members ens1f0,ens1f1
.RE
.fi
.PP
Replace a failing NIC without losing redundancy, then confirm:
.PP
.nf
.RS
bond\-manager swap\-member bond0 \-\-old ens1f0 \-\-new ens2f0
bond\-manager commit
.RE
.fi
.PP
Add a VLAN with its own address on top of an existing bond:
.PP
.nf
.RS
bond\-manager vlan add bond0 '100:ip4=10.0.100.10/24;gw4=10.0.100.1'
.RE
.fi
.PP
Health for monitoring (exit code 0/10/11) and JSON inventory:
.PP
.nf
.RS
bond\-manager status
bond\-manager \-\-json status > /tmp/bonds.json
bond\-manager status bond0 \-\-json      # just that bond, exit 0/10/11
.RE
.fi
.PP
Something went wrong \(em revert to the state before the last change:
.PP
.nf
.RS
bond\-manager rollback
.RE
.fi
.PP
Review an older snapshot before restoring it, then restore it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
bond\-manager snapshot list
bond\-manager snapshot diff 20260825\-141530
bond\-manager \-n snapshot restore 20260825\-141530   # rehearsal, writes nothing
bond\-manager snapshot restore 20260825\-141530
.RE
.fi
